From 217b6536512d7cdc6b40310f8390a3cfaf919b2c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 19:54:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] expand: a forwarded `:expr` fragment keeps its parentheses
 and stays one operand in proc-macro input

age 0.11.1 (kbs2 0.7.3) src/error.rs:385 `wfl!(f, "rec-excessive-work",
duration = (1 << (required - target)))`:
  MACRO<age::fl> error:0: Failed to find an impl of core::ops::bit::Shl<_,> for
  i32 with Output = fluent_bundle::types::FluentValue<'_>

`wfl!` -> age's `fl!` -> i18n-embed-fl 0.9.4 `fl!` (proc macro). The `$args:expr`
fragment is the assignment `duration = (1 << (required - target))`; the proc
macro parses `key = value` through it (syn looks through rustc's invisible
`Delimiter::None` group) and emits `args.insert("duration", #value.into())`.
mrustc hands a `:expr` fragment to a proc macro by printing the AST
(DumpAST_Node) and re-lexing it, and the AST had no trace of the source's
parentheses (the parser returns the inner node of `( e )`), so the macro's
output re-parsed as `1 << (required - target).into()`. The same loss hit
`stringify!($e)` of a parenthesised fragment and a `(self.f)(x)` call of
a field (printed as the method call `self.f(x)`).

- AST::ExprNode records "written in parentheses": parse/expr.cpp sets it for
  `( e )` (not tuples), clone copies it (ast/expr.cpp NEWNODE).
- The source-form printer (ast/dump.cpp) prints them once: every child goes
  through RustPrinter::visit(ExprNodeP&), paren_wrap and the cast / type
  annotation / call-object / if-let condition wrapping defer to the node's own,
  DumpAST_Node handles a parenthesised root.
- The bridge (expand/proc_macro.cpp) additionally parenthesises an operator
  fragment the source did not: BinOp (incl. ranges), prefix UniOp, Deref, Cast,
  type annotation, `return`/`break`/`continue` - what rustc's invisible group
  does for the macro's OUTPUT - while everything a proc macro takes apart or
  that binds tighter than its context goes as written: `key = value`, closures,
  blocks, `if`/`match`/loops, calls, method chains, fields, indexing, `x?`,
  `.await`, literals, paths, tuples, arrays, macro invocations.

Assisted-By: Claude Fable 5.1
